_main._4x3.jpg"},{"nm":"2x1","aspRto":2,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/14c1b62b-b787-43b2-8cb1-7b5fe6437294/082526_MK_SwiftObs_main._2x1.jpg"}],"rt":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/14c1b62b-b787-43b2-8cb1-7b5fe6437294/082526_MK_SwiftObs_main..jpg","src":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/14c1b62b-b787-43b2-8cb1-7b5fe6437294/082526_MK_SwiftObs_main..jpg","dsc":"NASA’s Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory, shown in this illustration, launched into Earth orbit in November 2004. The satellite investigates gamma-ray bursts, the most energetic explosions in the universe. Swift observes the sky in visible, ultraviolet, X-ray, and gamma-ray light. Its name reflects its ability to rapidly follow up on interesting objects in the sky. Swift also studies supernova explosions, star-shredding black holes in other galaxies, comets, stellar remnants called neutron stars, and other cosmic phenomena. In 2018, NASA renamed Swift in honor of the late Neil Gehrels, who helped develop the mission and served as its principal investigator for 13 years. This illustration provides an alternate view of the spacecraft."}},"title":"Nat Geo Recommends"},"key":"mnu","title":"Science & Nature","id":"natgeo-nav-module1-tile6-new1","ctaLink":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science"},{"tiles":[{"title":"Best of the World with Antoni Porowski","href":"https://www.nationalgeographic.com/tv/show/2737b748-75b9-473e-a066-eb21d0281482","img":{"crps":[{"nm":"raw","aspRto":1.7777777777777777,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/46cb0c99-abea-4352-88e2-b870656b1c2e/BestOfTheWorld_NetEffects_1280x720_clean.jpg"},{"nm":"16x9","aspRto":1.7777777777777777,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/46cb0c99-abea-4352-88e2-b870656b1c2e/BestOfTheWorld_NetEffects_1280x720_clean_16x9.jpg"},{"nm":"3x2","aspRto":1.5,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/46cb0c99-abea-4352-88e2-b870656b1c2e/BestOfTheWorld_NetEffects_1280x720_clean_3x2.jpg"},{"nm":"square","aspRto":1,"url":"https://i.natgeofe.com/n/46cb0c99-abea-4352-88e2-b870656b1c2e/BestOfTheWorld_NetEffect